--- !u!114 &-5322928012254498336
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "This template offers detailed examples for developing two distinct types of game modes. Depending on your specific requirements, it is entirely feasible to blend elements from both approaches to create more tailored, dynamic experiences.\n\n<b>Scene Based Game Modes</b> exist within the main networked scene at all times. This is good if you want data that is persistent between game modes (i.e. bringing an item from one game mode into another). \nYou can see examples for <b>Scene Based Game Modes</b> in the <b>Game Mode Sandbox</b> and <b>Game Mode Slingshot</b> prefabs.\n\n<b>Additively Loaded Game Modes</b> load and unload entire scenes additively for all users. This helps memory management by not keeping the persistent data around and decouples development workflow from a shared scene. This also makes porting existing tabletop games into this template much easier.\nYou can see an example of <b>Additively Loaded Game Modes</b> in the <b>Game Mode Chess</b> prefab."
